\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces There are two ways of calibrating the photodiode signal that will be generated by a change in optical path length between the two paths in the Mach-Zehnder. Method (a) is to apply a ramp signal to the PZT while simultaneously measuring the photodiode signals. The measurement of this is shown by the blue crosses, and the fit to this data is shown by the blue line. Method (b) is to measure the power in the local oscillator and signal beams when they are not interfering. From this, Equation \ref  {eq:MZ_chapter_base_equation} can be used to get the response of the Mach-Zehnder. This is shown by the red line. The blue and red data are independent of each other, within 20\% of each other and are in agreement with each other. The gradient of $V_{BHD}$ is used to get the conversion factor for beam power to path length change.\relax }}{12}{figure.caption.16}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterinaircalibration}{{1.5}{12}{There are two ways of calibrating the photodiode signal that will be generated by a change in optical path length between the two paths in the Mach-Zehnder. Method (a) is to apply a ramp signal to the PZT while simultaneously measuring the photodiode signals. The measurement of this is shown by the blue crosses, and the fit to this data is shown by the blue line. Method (b) is to measure the power in the local oscillator and signal beams when they are not interfering. From this, Equation \ref {eq:MZ_chapter_base_equation} can be used to get the response of the Mach-Zehnder. This is shown by the red line. The blue and red data are independent of each other, within 20\% of each other and are in agreement with each other. The gradient of $V_{BHD}$ is used to get the conversion factor for beam power to path length change.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Shot noise was measured at frequencies above 5500\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz. Below 5500\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, acoustic noise limited the MZ sensitivity. The peak at 6033\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz was injected to demonstrate the effect of a differential arm length modulation.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.18}}
\newlabel{fig:mzchapterfullnoisespectrum}{{1.6}{13}{Shot noise was measured at frequencies above 5500\,Hz. Below 5500\,Hz, acoustic noise limited the MZ sensitivity. The peak at 6033\,Hz was injected to demonstrate the effect of a differential arm length modulation.\relax }{figure.caption.18}{}}
